    "editorialMarkdown": "## Backtracking: choose, recurse, un-choose — with a shared buffer\n\nThe trick that allows the strict ordering to fall out for free is to explicitly enumerate **by size**. By iterating a target size `k` from `0` up to `n`, and only generating subsets of exactly length `k` by picking indices from left to right, the desired output order emerges naturally. Because the input arrives sorted, and the backtracking loop consumes indices in increasing order, the subsets will automatically satisfy the position-by-position requirements without needing any post-processing sort.\n\nA shared buffer array (`buf`) is reused at every level of the recursive call stack. The `pop()` operation at the end of the loop is critical: it \"un-chooses\" the element. When the recursive function returns, the buffer correctly reflects its state from before the recursive call, presenting a clean prefix for the next iteration.\n\nThe quiet mistake is appending the `buf` array directly to the output list rather than a copy of it. In languages where arrays are passed by reference, this results in the output list containing multiple pointers to the exact same underlying buffer. Because the backtracking process eventually pops the buffer back to empty on its way out, every row in the final answer will unexpectedly end up empty. You must append a clone or snapshot of the buffer.\n\nThe time complexity is O(n * 2^n), as there are 2^n subsets and copying each to the output takes up to O(n) time. Since the output itself is this large, the time is optimal. The auxiliary space complexity is O(n) for the shared recursion buffer and the call stack.",
