    "editorialMarkdown": "The intended approach uses Kahn's algorithm for topological sorting. We represent the tasks and their prerequisites as a directed graph and compute the indegree of each node. We use a queue to process all nodes with an indegree of 0. For each processed node, we decrement the indegree of its neighbors, and if a neighbor's indegree becomes 0, we add it to the queue. The pattern is topological sort. Time complexity is O(V + E) where V is the number of tasks and E is the number of prerequisites. Space complexity is O(V + E).\n\nThe one trap most solvers hit is constructing the graph edges in the wrong direction, leading to incorrect indegree calculations and failure to properly identify nodes with no prerequisites.",
