    "editorialMarkdown": "The intended approach uses backtracking to construct permutations step by step. We maintain an array of booleans to mark which positions in the original list have been used. At each step, we iterate through the unused items and recursively choose them. \n\nThe primary trap solvers hit is generating duplicate permutations and then trying to filter them out later, which is inefficient. Because the input list is sorted, identical values sit next to each other. We can prevent duplicates by enforcing that we only consume identical values in their original left-to-right order. If the current number is the same as the previous one, and the previous one was not used in the current branch, we skip the current number. Dropping the check that the previous number is unused will mistakenly prevent placing two identical numbers at all.\n\nThe time complexity is bounded by the number of unique permutations, which is O(n!) in the worst case. The space complexity is O(n) to track the used numbers and the call stack, where n is the number of elements.",
