    "editorialMarkdown": "The intended approach uses the \"at most\" trick with a sliding window. A sliding window struggles to directly count \"exactly `k`\" distinct characters because the condition is not monotonic—adding a character might make a valid window invalid, but removing a character might also do the same. Instead, we can write a helper function that counts substrings with \"at most `k`\" distinct characters, and compute the answer as `atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1)`.\n\nThe main trap most solvers hit is decrementing the frequency of an outgoing character in the sliding window's hash map without completely deleting its key when the count reaches zero. If the key remains with a value of zero, the length of the map still includes it, breaking the logic that tracks the number of distinct characters, which results in undercounting.\n\nThe time complexity is O(n) because each edge of the sliding window passes over the string of length n at most once. The space complexity is O(A) where A is the size of the alphabet used to maintain the counts.",
