    "editorialMarkdown": "The intended approach combines sorting with the two-pointer technique. First, you sort the array to group duplicate values. Then, you loop through the array and fix one number as the anchor. For the remainder of the array to the right of the anchor, you use two pointers (one starting at the left, one at the right) moving inward to find pairs that sum to the negative value of the anchor.\n\nThe trap most solvers hit is overcounting duplicate triplets. Fixing this requires two separate skip checks. The first skips duplicate anchor values, ensuring the same starting number doesn't rediscover the same triplets. The second skip occurs after a valid triplet is found: the left pointer must be advanced past any adjacent duplicates to avoid counting the exact same triplet using a different copy of the second value. Missing either skip check will artificially inflate the final count.\n\nThe time complexity is O(n^2), as fixing the anchor takes O(n) and the two-pointer scan takes O(n) for each anchor, which dominates the O(n log n) sorting step. The space complexity is O(1) if sorting is performed in-place.",
