    "editorialMarkdown": "## Adjacency lists preserve local edges\n\nAn adjacency list gives every vertex its own compact list of outgoing vertices, avoiding an n-by-n matrix for sparse graphs. Inserting a new edge then sorting or ordered-inserting that one list makes neighbors deterministic and supplies the same ascending order required by traversal methods.\n\nThe quiet mistake is treating edges as undirected without being told; adding a reverse edge makes neighbors and later traversal unexpectedly reach vertices that should be disconnected.\n\nAdding an edge costs O(deg(u)) here to check and keep its list ordered; neighbors costs O(deg(u)) to copy, and graph storage is O(n + e).",
