  "hash-set-add-is-idempotent": {
    "promptMarkdown": "Write a custom set data structure from the ground up, avoiding any built-in set types.\n\nYour `HashSet` will track unique integers utilizing an array of bucket lists. You must provide `add(x)` to include an element, `contains(x)` to return a boolean indicating presence, `remove(x)` to strike an element, and `size()` to state the total number of distinct elements.\n\nCrucially, the set must be strictly idempotent. If `add(x)` is called with an element already present in the set, the internal state and the result of `size()` must not change. Similarly, calling `remove(x)` for an item that isn't stored must do absolutely nothing to the size count. \n\n**Operation log**\n\nThe first operation is the constructor. Print `null` for it and for every void method. Every other operation prints its return value. Membership checks print `true` or `false`.\n\n**Constraints**\n- Elements are integers.\n- Total unique elements will not exceed typical memory limits.\n\n**Example 1**\n```\ninput:\n8\nHashSet\nadd 5\nadd 5\nsize\ncontains 5\nremove 5\ncontains 5\nsize\noutput:\nnull\nnull\nnull\n1\ntrue\nnull\nfalse\n0\n```\nAdding 5 twice results in a size of 1, demonstrating that the duplicate addition was ignored.\n\n**Example 2**\n```\ninput:\n4\nHashSet\nremove 3\nsize\ncontains 3\noutput:\nnull\nnull\n0\nfalse\n```\nRemoving an element that was never added leaves the set size safely at zero.\n\n**Follow-up:** What logic is required during insertion to guarantee idempotency in a bucketed structure?",
    "editorialMarkdown": "## Membership check before insert\n\nA hash set is a hash table that stores only keys. Hashing sends `x` to a bucket; the bucket holds every value that landed there, so `contains` is a short scan of one bucket rather than the whole set. Idempotence is not automatic — you get it by scanning that bucket first and appending only when the value is absent. That is also why the stored count must be maintained by hand: increment only on a real insert, decrement only on a real removal.\n\nThe quiet mistake is counting instead of checking. Appending unconditionally and incrementing the counter gives right answers from `contains` forever — the duplicate is in the bucket, so it is found — while `size()` slowly drifts upward. Nothing about the wrong number is visible until a test asks for it. The mirror case is `remove` of an absent value decrementing the count anyway. Both are one comparison away from correct.\n\nWith buckets kept short, `add`, `contains` and `remove` are O(1) on average and O(n) if everything collides into one bucket; `size` is O(1). Space is O(n).",
    "promoteSamples": [],
    "model": "gemini-3.1-pro-low",
    "date": "2026-09-16"
  },
