    "editorialMarkdown": "The intended approach requires maintaining both a `head` and a `tail` pointer to support O(1) insertions at both ends, along with a `size` counter to easily return the number of elements and validate state. `removeFirst` is O(1), while `get` and `toArray` require traversing the list, taking O(n) time. The space complexity for the list itself is O(n), and `toArray` requires O(n) auxiliary space to construct the new array. \n\nThe most common trap solvers hit is failing to cleanly manage the `tail` pointer when the list becomes empty. When `removeFirst` removes the last remaining element, the `tail` must be updated to `null` to prevent subsequent `addLast` operations from implicitly reattaching the removed node or failing entirely. Additionally, keeping the element count as the source of truth is crucial; treating an empty structure as though it has a valid first or last slot often passes simple test cases but fails after a long sequence of removals.",
