    "promptMarkdown": "Implement a Disjoint-Set (Union-Find) data structure to track the number of isolated components as connections are made.\n\nStart with `n` isolated elements (0 to `n - 1`). You must maintain the current number of connected components. Decrease the component count only when a union operation successfully joins two elements that are not already in the same component. A repeated union of elements already in the same component should not change the count.\n\nImplement the `UnionFind` class:\n* `UnionFind(int n)` Initializes the object with `n` elements, each in its own component.\n* `void unite(int a, int b)` Connects the components containing elements `a` and `b`.\n* `int find(int x)` Returns the representative (root) of the component containing `x`. Use path compression.\n* `bool connected(int a, int b)` Returns `true` if elements `a` and `b` are in the same component, `false` otherwise.\n* `int count()` Returns the current number of distinct components.\n\n**Constraints**\n- `1 <= n <= 10^5`\n- `0 <= a, b, x < n`\n- At most `10^5` calls will be made in total to `unite`, `find`, `connected`, and `count`.\n\n**Example 1**\n```\ninput:\n11\nUnionFind 6\ncount\nunite 0 1\nunite 1 2\nunite 0 2\ncount\nunite 3 4\nunite 4 5\ncount\nconnected 2 5\nunite 2 5\noutput:\nnull\n6\nnull\nnull\nnull\n4\nnull\nnull\n2\nfalse\nnull\n```\nWe start with 6 components. Joining 0, 1, and 2 reduces the count to 4. Joining 3, 4, and 5 reduces it to 2.\n\n**Example 2**\n```\ninput:\n4\nUnionFind 3\ncount\nconnected 0 2\nfind 2\noutput:\nnull\n3\nfalse\n2\n```\nElements 0 and 2 are initially in separate components.\n\n**Follow-up:** Can you optimize `unite` and `find` to run in nearly constant amortized time?",
    "editorialMarkdown": "## Count successful merges only\n\nA disjoint-set forest begins with `n` individual components. Every successful root-to-root union reduces the total component count by exactly one. An attempted union where both elements already share the same root represents a cycle in the connectivity graph and does not decrease the count. Maintaining a running count field allows `count()` to return immediately in O(1) time without scanning all elements.\n\nThe most common trap is decrementing the total component count before verifying whether the elements' roots already match. Duplicate edges will then quietly cause the reported component total to become too small.\n\nThe `count` operation takes O(1) time. The `unite`, `find`, and `connected` operations execute in O(alpha(n)) amortized time, where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function, due to the combination of union by rank and path compression. The parent and rank arrays require O(n) space.",
